## Objective

The purpose of this analysis is to quantify the minimum torsional stiffness required for the 2027 monocoque so that structural compliance does not significantly reduce the intended suspension response.

The design target is for the overall vehicle stiffness to retain **90%** of the ideal stiffness set by the suspension spring parameters.

This means the monocoque and rigid suspension structures must be sufficiently stiff that their combined compliance reduces the intended suspension response by no more than 10%.

---

## Torsional Stiffness Definition

Torsional stiffness is defined as the applied torque divided by the resulting angular deflection:

$$
K = \frac{T}{\theta}
$$

where:

- $K$ is the torsional stiffness,
- $T$ is the applied torque,
- $\theta$ is the angular deflection.

For a test in which a vertical force $F$ is applied at a lever arm $L$:

$$
T = FL
$$

If the two sides of the structure vertically displace by a combined amount $\Delta$, the twist angle may be approximated as:

$$
\theta
=
\tan^{-1}
\left(
\frac{\Delta}{L}
\right)
$$

Therefore, the measured torsional stiffness is:

$$
K
=
\frac{FL}
{
\tan^{-1}
\left(
\frac{\Delta}{L}
\right)
}
$$

For sufficiently small angular deflections:

$$
\tan^{-1}
\left(
\frac{\Delta}{L}
\right)
\approx
\frac{\Delta}{L}
$$

which gives:

$$
K
\approx
\frac{FL^2}{\Delta}
$$

This small-angle form is useful because it directly relates the measured vertical deflection to torsional stiffness.

---

## Equivalent Torsional Wheel Rate

The physical suspension springs are linear springs, while the monocoque and suspension structural stiffnesses are expressed as torsional stiffnesses.

The wheel rate must therefore be converted into an equivalent torsional stiffness.

For one wheel located a distance $L$ from the vehicle centerline:

$$
K_{\mathrm{wheel,torsion}}
\approx
K_{\mathrm{wheel,linear}}L^2
$$

This relationship assumes consistent units and angular displacement measured in radians.

The torsional wheel rate used as the normalization factor for the 2026 vehicle was selected from the suspension calculations:

$$
K_{\mathrm{wheel}}
=
171.92
\ \mathrm{N\,m/deg}
$$

This represents the intended compliant behavior of the suspension springs during the one-wheel-bump loading case.

---

## Series Stiffness Model

When compliant components act in series, their angular deflections add:

$$
\theta_{\mathrm{total}}
=
\theta_{\mathrm{wheel}}
+
\theta_{\mathrm{susp}}
+
\theta_{\mathrm{mono}}
$$

Because:

$$
\theta = \frac{T}{K}
$$

the total vehicle stiffness is:

$$
\boxed{
\frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{vehicle}}}
=
\frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{wheel}}}
+
\frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{susp}}}
+
\frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{mono}}}
}
$$

This equation shows that the softest element has the greatest influence on the overall vehicle stiffness.

---

## Separating Monocoque and Suspension Compliance

The hub-to-hub torsional stiffness test includes deformation from both the monocoque and the suspension structure.

Therefore:

$$
\frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{hub}}}
=
\frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{mono}}}
+
\frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{susp}}}
$$

The measured values were:

$$
K_{\mathrm{hub}}
=
1907
\ \mathrm{N\,m/deg}
$$

and:

$$
K_{\mathrm{mono}}
=
3364
\ \mathrm{N\,m/deg}
$$

Solving for the suspension structural stiffness:

$$
\frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{susp}}}
=
\frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{hub}}}
-
\frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{mono}}}
$$

Therefore:

$$
K_{\mathrm{susp}}
=
\left(
\frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{hub}}}
-
\frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{mono}}}
\right)^{-1}
$$

Substituting the measured values:

$$
K_{\mathrm{susp}}
=
\left(
\frac{1}{1907}
-
\frac{1}{3364}
\right)^{-1}
$$

which gives:

$$
K_{\mathrm{susp}}
\approx
4403
\ \mathrm{N\,m/deg}
$$

Thus, the suspension structure is somewhat stiffer than the monocoque, although both are substantially stiffer than the equivalent wheel rate.

---

## Normalized Stiffness Values

To make the results independent of the absolute wheel rate, each stiffness is divided by the equivalent torsional wheel rate:

$$
K_i'
=
\frac{K_i}{K_{\mathrm{wheel}}}
$$

By definition:

$$
K_{\mathrm{wheel}}' = 1
$$

### Normalized Monocoque Stiffness

The normalized monocoque stiffness is:

$$
K_{\mathrm{mono}}'
=
\frac{3364}{171.92}
$$

Therefore:

$$
K_{\mathrm{mono}}'
\approx
19.57
$$

### Normalized Suspension Structural Stiffness

The normalized suspension structural stiffness is:

$$
K_{\mathrm{susp}}'
=
\frac{4403}{171.92}
$$

Therefore:

$$
K_{\mathrm{susp}}'
\approx
25.61
$$

The measured structural stiffness values were therefore:

$$
\boxed{
K_{\mathrm{mono}}' = 19.57,
\qquad
K_{\mathrm{susp}}' = 25.61
}
$$

In physical terms, the monocoque was approximately 19.6 times as stiff as the equivalent wheel rate, while the suspension structure was approximately 25.6 times as stiff.

---

## Determining the Monocoque Stiffness Target

The ideal rigid-structure vehicle has no compliance in either the monocoque or the suspension structure.

Its normalized stiffness is therefore controlled entirely by the wheel rate:

$$
K_{\mathrm{vehicle,rigid}}' = 1
$$

The target is for the actual vehicle to retain 90% of this ideal stiffness:

$$
K_{\mathrm{vehicle,target}}' = 0.90
$$

The normalized series-stiffness equation is:

$$
\frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{vehicle}}'}
=
\frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{wheel}}'}
+
\frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{susp}}'}
+
\frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{mono}}'}
$$

Substituting the target vehicle stiffness, normalized wheel rate, and measured suspension stiffness gives:

$$
\frac{1}{0.90}
=
1
+
\frac{1}{25.61}
+
\frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}'}
$$

Rearranging:

$$
\frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}'}
=
\frac{1}{0.90}
-
1
-
\frac{1}{25.61}
$$

Therefore:

$$
K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}'
=
\left(
\frac{1}{0.90}
-
1
-
\frac{1}{25.61}
\right)^{-1}
$$

which gives:

$$
K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}'
\approx
13.88
$$

Converting back to dimensional torsional stiffness:

$$
K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}
=
K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}'
K_{\mathrm{wheel}}
$$

Substituting the calculated normalized target and the torsional wheel rate:

$$
K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}
=
13.88
\left(
171.92
\ \mathrm{N\,m/deg}
\right)
$$

Therefore:

$$
\boxed{
K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}
\approx
2386
\ \mathrm{N\,m/deg}
}
$$

Small differences from the spreadsheet result may occur because of rounding in the displayed input values.

---

## Design Interpretation

The calculated minimum monocoque target is approximately:

$$
K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}
\approx
2.39 \times 10^3
\ \mathrm{N\,m/deg}
$$

The measured monocoque stiffness was:

$$
K_{\mathrm{mono,measured}}
=
3364
\ \mathrm{N\,m/deg}
$$

The stiffness margin relative to the target is therefore:

$$
\text{Stiffness margin}
=
\frac{
K_{\mathrm{mono,measured}}
-
K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}
}{
K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}
}
$$

Substituting the values:

$$
\text{Stiffness margin}
=
\frac{3364-2386}{2386}
$$

Therefore:

$$
\text{Stiffness margin}
\approx
0.41
$$

or:

$$
\boxed{
41\%
}
$$

Thus, the measured monocoque was approximately 41% stiffer than the calculated minimum target.

This indicates that the monocoque provides a sufficiently rigid platform for the suspension while avoiding the unnecessary mass associated with pursuing infinite structural stiffness.

