## Conclusion

The analysis treats the suspension springs, suspension structure, and monocoque as torsional springs acting in series.

The design objective is not for the monocoque to provide 90% of the total stiffness. Instead, the complete vehicle should retain 90% of the stiffness it would have if the monocoque and suspension structure were perfectly rigid.

Using a normalized wheel rate of 1, a suspension structural stiffness of 25.61, and a target vehicle stiffness of 0.90, the required normalized monocoque stiffness was calculated as:

$$
K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}'
\approx
13.88
$$

This corresponds to a dimensional target of approximately:

$$
\boxed{
K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}
\approx
2386
\ \mathrm{N\,m/deg}
}
$$

The measured monocoque stiffness of:

$$
3364
\ \mathrm{N\,m/deg}
$$

exceeded this requirement, demonstrating that the structure was sufficiently stiff relative to the suspension system.

